- My follow up question)You just pass users object to process users..how it works then ?

Because processUsers is created by newPipe(...), and newPipe returns a function that accepts the input later.

Here’s the idea:

const newPipe = (...fns) => {
  return (value) => fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value);
};

What happens step by step
When you do:

const processUsers = newPipe(
  filterActiveUsers,
  fullNameOfUser,
  upperCase,
  sortAlphabetically
);
you are creating a new function equivalent to:

const processUsers = (value) => {
  return [filterActiveUsers, fullNameOfUser, upperCase, sortAlphabetically]
    .reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), value);
};
So later, when you call:

processUsers(users);
that users array becomes the starting value.

How reduce applies the pipeline
If users is the input, then this happens:

filterActiveUsers(users)
result goes into fullNameOfUser(...)
result goes into upperCase(...)
result goes into sortAlphabetically(...)

